    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nAbsence of d-orbitals in second period elements.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nBoron valence shell configuration: 2s2 2p1 (n = 2).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nOnly four valence orbitals (one 2s and three 2p) are available for bonding.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Boron belongs to the second period of the periodic table.\n2. It has only four valence orbitals: one 2s and three 2p orbitals.\n3. Because it has no vacant d-orbitals, it cannot expand its valence shell beyond an octet.\n4. Therefore, its maximum covalency is strictly 4 (as in [BF4]- and [B(OH)4]-).\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 4\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nAluminium can exhibit a covalency of 6 (e.g. [AlF6]3-) due to availability of vacant 3d orbitals, but Boron cannot.",
