    "question": "Identify the valid statement: What is the product formed when diborane is methylated with trimethylborane?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Me4B2H2 (tetramethyldiborane)",
      "B": "Me6B2 (hexamethyldiborane)",
      "C": "Me5B2H",
      "D": "Me3B2H3"
    },
    "optionsList": [
      "Me4B2H2 (tetramethyldiborane)",
      "Me6B2 (hexamethyldiborane)",
      "Me5B2H",
      "Me3B2H3"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nTerminal vs bridging hydrogen reactivity in diborane.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nReaction of B2H6 with BMe3.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nOnly terminal B-H bonds can be substituted by methyl groups; bridging hydrogens cannot be methylated.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. In diborane, the four terminal hydrogen atoms are normally bonded and can be replaced by alkyl groups.\n2. The two bridging hydrogens participate in 3c-2e banana bonds and cannot be substituted without rupturing the diborane cage.\n3. Therefore, methylation reaches a maximum substitution of four methyl groups, producing Me4B2H2.\n4. Hexamethyldiborane does not exist.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Me4B2H2 (tetramethyldiborane)\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nBridging B-H-B bonds are resistant to terminal alkyl substitution.",
    "solutionStructured": {
      "concept": "Terminal vs bridging hydrogen reactivity in diborane.",
      "given": "Reaction of B2H6 with BMe3.",
      "formula": "Only terminal B-H bonds can be substituted by methyl groups; bridging hydrogens cannot be methylated.",
      "stepByStep": "1. In diborane, the four terminal hydrogen atoms are normally bonded and can be replaced by alkyl groups.\n2. The two bridging hydrogens participate in 3c-2e banana bonds and cannot be substituted without rupturing the diborane cage.\n3. Therefore, methylation reaches a maximum substitution of four methyl groups, producing Me4B2H2.\n4. Hexamethyldiborane does not exist.",
      "finalAnswer": "Option A: Me4B2H2 (tetramethyldiborane)",
      "whyOtherOptionsWrong": "Bridging B-H-B bonds are resistant to terminal alkyl substitution."
    },
