    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nPassivity of aluminium in oxidizing acids.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nReaction of Al with concentrated HNO3.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nFormation of an impermeable, chemically inert surface film of Al2O3.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Concentrated nitric acid is a potent oxidizing agent.\n2. When aluminium metal is dipped in concentrated HNO3, a thin, tenacious, microscopic layer of aluminium oxide (Al2O3) rapidly forms on the surface.\n3. This oxide layer isolates the underlying metal from further acid attack.\n4. Hence, aluminium becomes passive and is rendered unreactive.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Aluminium\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nThis passivity enables transport and storage of concentrated nitric acid in aluminium containers.",
